l = 118.97 + 11.90 = 130.87\n```\n\nBut the test expects 130.86. Looking at the test comments:\n```\n// subtotal = 3*29.99 + 49.99 = 139.96\n// discount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n// after discount = 118.966\n// tax = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\n// total = 130.8626 -> rounded to 130.86\n```\n\nThey don't round intermediate values. The code currently rounds `discountAmount`, `afterDiscount`, and `taxAmount` to 2 decimal places. But the test expects them NOT to be rounded as aggressively. \n\nLet me re-check the formula:\n```\nsubtotal = 139.96\ndiscountAmount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\nafterDiscount = 139.96 - 20.994 = 118.966\ntaxAmount = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\ntotal = 118.966 + 11.8966 = 130.8626\n```\n\nThen `total = Math.round(130.8626 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(13086.26) / 100 = 13086 / 100 = 130.86`\n\nSo the issue is that the current code rounds `discountAmount` and `afterDiscount` to 2 decimal places before calculating the next step, leading to accumulated rounding errors.\n\nThe fix: only round the final `total`, not the intermediate values.\n\nActually looking more carefully:\n\n```javascript\nconst discountAmount = Math.round(subtotal * (discountPercent / 100) * 100) / 100;\nconst afterDiscount = Math.round((subtotal - discountAmount) * 100) / 100;\nconst taxAmount = Math.round(afterDiscount * taxRate * 100) / 100;\n```\n\nThe problem is double-rounding. When `discountAmount = 20.994`, rounding to `20.99` truncates precision. Then `subtotal - discountAmount = 139.96 - 20.99 = 118.97` but should be `139.96 - 20.994 = 118.966`.\n\nThe fix: keep precision in intermediate calculations, only round the final result:\n\n```javascript\nconst discountAmount = subtotal * (discountPercent / 100);\nconst afterDiscount = subtotal - discountAmount;\nconst taxAmount = afterDiscount * taxRate;\nconst total = Math.round((afterDiscount + taxAmount) * 100) / 100;\n```\n\nBut the discountAmount and taxAmount values in the return object should probably still be rounded...\n\nActua