urns a copy, and the item access... Wait, `order` is the result of `this.repo.findById(id)`. Let me look at the issue more carefully.\n\nActually, the error `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')` on line 78 is:\n```typescript\nitems: order.items.map((i) => ({\n```\n\nSo `order` is defined but `order.items` is undefined. This means the order was saved without items.\n\nLooking at `createOrder`:\n```typescript\nthis.repo.create({\n    id,\n    customerId,\n    items: [...items],\n    status: 'pending',\n    total: 0,\n    discount,\n    taxRate,\n    createdAt: Date.now(),\n    updatedAt: Date.now(),\n});\n```\n\nThe order is created with `items: [...items]`. Then later updated:\n```typescript\nthis.repo.update(id, {\n    status: 'confirmed',\n    total: payment.amount,\n});\n```\n\nNow look at `OrderRepository.update()`:\n```typescript\nupdate(id: string, updates: Partial<Order>): Order | undefined {\n    const existing = this.store.get(id);\n    if (!existing) return undefined;\n    const updated = { ...updates, id, updatedAt: Date.now() } as Order;\n    this.store.set(id, updated);\n    return { ...updated };\n}\n```\n\nThis creates a new object `{ ...updates, id, updatedAt }` without spreading `existing`! So ALL existing fields (including `items`) are lost when `update()` is called. This means after `update(id, { status: 'confirmed', total: payment.amount })`, the stored order has only `status`, `total`, `id`, and `updatedAt` — no `items`, `customerId`, `discount`, `taxRate`, `createdAt`, etc.\n\nThis matches the `OrderRepository > updates order preserves other fields` test failure:\n```\nexpected undefined to be 'C1'\n```\nBecause `customerId` was lost during update.\n\nSo the fix for `OrderRepository.update()` is:\n```typescript\nconst updated = { ...existing, ...updates, id, updatedAt: Date.now() } as Order;\n```\n\nThis also explains the `items.map` issue in `cancelOrder` - after `createOrder` calls `update` (which destroys `items`), the order n