itory, InventoryService, etc. So each test has its own repo. The handlers from previous tests reference OLD repos that no longer have the stock set for the new event. So:\n\nHandler from test 1: repo has stock set for SKU-1 = 10 (from test 1's setup), but test 1 reserved 3. So available=7. When test 4's event comes with qty 3, handler 1 tries to reserve 3 from available=7 → succeeds (7 >= 3). OK that works.\n\nHandler from test 2: repo has stock = 2 (from test 2's setup). Tries to reserve 3 (from test 4's event) → fails (2 < 3). THROWS ERROR.\n\nThat's the bug! Handler from test 2 fails because its repo only has 2 in stock.\n\nBut wait, test 2 was \"throws when stock is insufficient\" - it set SKU-1=2 and tried to reserve 5. That failed. So test 2's repo has available=2 (since the rollback logic in handleOrderCreated... let me check: when reserve fails, it checks `if (!success)` and throws. The rollback is for PREVIOUS reservations in the same batch. Since it tries to reserve 5 from 2 and fails immediately (no previous reservations), there's nothing to rollback. So repo still has available=2, reserved=0.\n\nHandler from test 2 (for test 4's event): tries to reserve SKU-1 qty 3 from available=2 → fails → Error(\"Insufficient stock for product SKU-1\").\n\nSo the root cause is that EventBus uses global handlers and tests don't clean up after themselves. The fix should be to call `bus.clear()` or track handlers properly.\n\nBut wait, the simplest fix would be to ensure that each test properly cleans up. But the constraint says \"Do not modify any test files.\"\n\nSo the fix must be in the source code. Options:\n1. Make EventBus non-global (instance-level handlers instead of global)\n2. Add cleanup in service constructors or somewhere\n\nActually, the cleanest fix is option 1: make the EventBus handlers instance-level rather than module-level. But looking at the code, the EventBus IS designed to be used by creating instances, but the handlers are global. This seems like a design choice to allow cross-instance communication, but it causes test pollution.\n\nFixing this would mean changing from `const globalHandlers` to `this.handlers` instance-level. But this could break the system's intended behavior.\n\nActually, looking at the code flow:\n1. EventBus handlers are global\n2. Multiple EventBus instances can be created\n3. When any instance emits, all globally registered handlers fire\n\nThis is a bug/feature. The tests expect isolated EventBus instances. The fix should make EventBus have instance-level handlers.\n\nLet me fix this by changing the Map to be instance-level. This will isolate tests properly.\n\n```typescript\nexport class EventBus {\n    private handlers: Map<string, EventHandler[]> = new Map();\n\n    on(event: string, handler: EventHandler): void {\n        const existing = this.handlers.get(event) ?? [];\n        existing.push(handler);\n        this.handlers.set(event, existing);\n    }\n\n    off(event: string, handler: EventHandler): void {\n        const existing = this.handlers.get(event);\n        if (!existing) return;\n        this.handlers.set(\n            event,\n            existing.filter((h) => h !== handler)\n        );\n    }\n\n    async emit(event: string, data: unknown): Promise<void> {\n        const handlers = this.handlers.get(event) ?? [];\n        for (const handler of handlers) {\n            await handler(data);\n        }\n    }\n\n    clear(): void {\n        this.handlers.clear();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 5. `OrderService > cancels a confirmed order` - `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')`\n\nThis is the `cancelOrder` method at line 78:\n```typescript\nitems: order.items.map((i) => ({\n```\n\nSince `order` is from `repo.findById(id)`, and the `update` bug destroyed the order data, `order.items` is undefined.\n\nThis will be fixed by fixing the `OrderRepository.update()` method.\n\n### 6. `OrderService > releases stock when order is cancelled` - `Insufficient stock`\n\nThis