his));\n```\n\nSo accumulated inventory handlers for CREATED cause the \"Insufficient stock\" error when a later test emits CREATED.\n\nAnd the orders test `beforeEach` creates everything fresh but doesn't clear the global handler state. So by test 4 (releases stock when cancelled), there are 3+ inventory handlers for CREATED, and each tries to reserve from its own repo. One of the earlier repos (from \"refuses to cancel a non-confirmed order\") doesn't have stock set.\n\nWait, \"creates an order and confirms it\" (test 1): before each, sets stock SKU-1 = 10, then creates order. Handler runs, reserves 2 from available=10. Repo: available=8, reserved=2.\n\n\"reserves stock when order is created\" (test 2): fresh beforeEach, sets stock SKU-1 = 10, creates order with qty 3. Handler runs successfully. Repo: available=7, reserved=3.\n\nBut there are now 2 handlers for CREATED. When test 2 emits CREATED, handler 1 (from test 1's setup) tries to reserve SKU-1 qty 3. Handler 1 uses test 1's repo which has available=8 (after test 1's own reservation). 8 >= 3, so it reserves 3 more. Now test 1's repo has available=5, reserved=5.\n\nThen handler 2 (from test 2) runs and reserves from test 2's repo: available=7, reserved=3.\n\nSo test 2 passes because both handlers succeed. But the handler registrations keep accumulating.\n\nTest 3 (cancel a confirmed order): fresh beforeEach, sets stock SKU-1=10, creates order with qty 2. Three handlers now. Handler 1: test 1's repo available=5, reserves 2 → available=3, reserved=7. Handler 2: test 2's repo available=7, reserves 2 → available=5, reserved=5. Handler 3: test 3's repo available=10, reserves 2 → available=8, reserved=2. All succeed. But the items MAP issue kills the test.\n\nWait, test 3 fails with `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')` - that's the `cancelOrder` method crashing because `order.items` is undefined (due to the update bug). The `createOrder` actually succeeds (because it creates the order first, then emits, then charges, then updates). The update bug doesn't prevent `createOrder` from completing - it just destroys the stored order data.\n\nOK so let me just fix the bugs:\n\n**Bug 1: OrderRepository.update() doesn't spread existing fields**\n```javascript\nconst updated = { ...existing, ...updates, id, updatedAt: Date.now() } as Order;\n```\n\n**Bug 2: EventBus uses global handlers that accumulate**\nChange to instance-level handlers.\n\n**Bug 3: BillingService.handleRefund not bound**\nChange `this.handleRefund` to `this.handleRefund.bind(this)`.\n\n**Bug 4: calculateOrderTotal double-rounding**\nRemove intermediate rounding.\n\nLet me verify that fixing all of these will resolve the 12 failures:\n\n1. ✓ OrderRepository > updates order preserves other fields - BUG 1 fix\n2. ✓ calculateOrderTotal > handles multiple items - BUG 4 fix\n3. ✓ BillingService > issues refund - BUG 3 fix (plus BUG 1 for order test context)\n4. ✓ BillingService > sends refund notification - BUG 3 fix\n5. ✓ InventoryService > releases stock on order.cancelled - BUG 2 fix (global handlers)\n6. ✓ InventoryService > reserves stock for concurrent orders independently - BUG 2 fix\n7. ✓ OrderService > cancels a confirmed order - BUG 1 fix\n8. ✓ OrderService > releases stock when order is cancelled - BUG 2 fix (+ BUG 1 for items)\n9. ✓ OrderService > refuses to cancel a non-confirmed order - BUG 1 fix\n10. ✓ OrderService > refunds a cancelled order within 30 days - BUG 1 fix\n11. ✓ OrderService > allows refund on exactly day 30 - BUG 1 fix\n12. ✓ OrderService > rejects refund after 30-day window - BUG 2 fix\n\nLet me also think about the \"allows refund on exactly day 30\" test. The issue is `expected undefined to be 'refunded'`. The test:\n```typescript\norderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs });\nawait orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'late cancel');\n```\n\nThe `orderRepo.update` call with the BUG 1 would store only `{ createdAt, id, updatedAt }` in the repo. Then `cancelOrder` calls `repo.findById(id)` which returns an order with no `items` → `items.map` crashes. With BUG 1 fixed, `cancelOrder` works and returns the order. Then `refundOrder` checks status === 'cancelled', which should be true. So it proceeds to check refund window:\n```typescript\nconst daysSinceCreation = Math.floor(\n    (Date.now() - order.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24)\n);\nif (daysSinceCreation >= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS) {\n    throw new Error(...);\n}\n```\n\nThe test sets createdAt = Date.now() - 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000 (exactly 30 days). So `daysSinceCreation = Math.floor(30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000 / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24)) = Math.floor(30) = 30`. The condition checks `daysSinceCreation >= 30`, which is true (30 >= 30). So it throws an error!\n\nBut the test expects the refund to succeed. The condition should be `daysSinceCreation > REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS` or `>= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS`?\n\nThe test says \"allows refund on exactly day 30\" and sets createdAt to exactly 30 days ago. So the refund should be allowed if it's been LESS than 30 days, or exactly 30 days.\n\nThe current code: `if (daysSinceCreation >= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS)` → 30 >= 30 → true → throws.\n\nThe fix: change to `if (daysSinceCreation > REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS)` or `if (daysSinceCreation >= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS + 1)` or similar.\n\nActually, \"within 30 days\" means daysSinceCreation < 30, i.e., the order was placed less than 30 days ago, or exactly up to 30 days. So `daysSinceCreation >= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS` should reject if it's been 30 or more days.\n\nBut the test expects \"allows refund on exactly day 30\". Let me re-read:\n```javascript\nit('allows refund on exactly day 30', async () => {\n    ...\n    const thirtyDaysMs = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;\n    orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs });\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'late cancel');\n    const refunded = await orderService.refundOrder('O1');\n    expect(refunded?.status).toBe('refunded');\n});\n```\n\nAnd:\n``